// NEGATIVE TEST -- a top-level initialiser must HAVE a value (N3-09).
//
// THE RULE THIS FIXTURE CLOSES WAS SYNTACTIC. It said "a literal, or operators
// over literals", and `1 / 0` is exactly that: the SHAPE is legal and the VALUE
// does not exist. So the expression passed every check the compiler had and
// reached the C compiler, which answered
// "initializer element is not a compile-time constant" at the emitted line --
// a diagnostic about generated code, for a division the author wrote.
//
// The initialiser is EVALUATED now, over the same closed list the parser
// accepts. Nothing new became writable; what changed is that a form with no
// value is refused by name instead of being passed on.
const X: i64 = 1 / 0;

fn main() {
    print_int(X);
}
